1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:11,439
Today, some 4,000 Americans across the country are working on a project known as the Clinch River Breeder Reactor Plant.

2
00:00:14,060 --> 00:00:17,960
The plant site is right here in Oak Ridge, Tennessee.

3
00:00:19,900 --> 00:00:22,280
You can see the Clinch River there at that bluff.

4
00:00:25,220 --> 00:00:28,500
The control room will be here where I'm standing.

5
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:36,420
With the resolution of licensing, construction could begin on the nation's first large-scale demonstration breeder in 1981.

6
00:00:37,020 --> 00:00:39,820
The plant could start producing electricity in 1988.

7
00:00:40,920 --> 00:00:47,179
The 350 megawatts of electricity from the Clinch River plant will be transmitted along these power lines,

8
00:00:47,819 --> 00:00:52,960
bringing enough electricity to 200,000 homes in the Tennessee Valley Authority service area.

9
00:00:53,399 --> 00:00:58,480
We're not turning earth here yet, but we've come a long way since the inception of the project.

10
00:00:58,600 --> 00:01:06,500
This report focuses on that progress and why we call the breeder a step toward energy independence.

11
00:01:06,600 --> 00:01:26,019
In fact, 753 utilities from across the country have pledged $257 million to build and operate this plant

12
00:01:26,119 --> 00:01:28,480
because they believe the Clinch River project is the best way to do it.

13
00:01:28,580 --> 00:01:34,680
This is the next logical step the United States must take in order to have a commercially feasible breeder ready when America needs it.

14
00:01:35,420 --> 00:01:40,219
This venture remains the largest industry commitment ever made for a single research and development project.

15
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:45,300
More than likely, a utility in your area belongs to the partnership of utilities

16
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:48,960
known as the Breeder Reactor Corporation formed to support the project.

17
00:01:54,759 --> 00:01:58,200
That brings us to the question, what is a breeder?

18
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:04,719
A breeder is a type of nuclear reactor that produces more fuel than it uses as it generates electricity.

19
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:06,320
How does it do this?

20
00:02:06,679 --> 00:02:07,640
Well, it's not magic.

21
00:02:08,539 --> 00:02:12,259
The breeder uses plutonium-239 and uranium-238.

22
00:02:12,979 --> 00:02:14,759
Plutonium is the fissionable material.

23
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,120
Fissionable means that it can split apart.

24
00:02:17,859 --> 00:02:18,980
Here, let me show you.

25
00:02:20,080 --> 00:02:25,480
When a neutron hits an atom of plutonium-239,

26
00:02:25,880 --> 00:02:28,460
it splits apart and creates a nuclear reactor.

27
00:02:28,579 --> 00:02:29,479
This doesn't require a lot of heat.

28
00:02:29,899 --> 00:02:34,359
The heat is used to boil water, to make steam, to produce electricity.

29
00:02:35,239 --> 00:02:38,879
Also, we get three new neutrons.

30
00:02:39,619 --> 00:02:41,239
Remember, we started with just one.

31
00:02:42,039 --> 00:02:42,939
Now we have three.

32
00:02:43,799 --> 00:02:46,620
One of these neutrons keeps our chain reaction going.

33
00:02:47,499 --> 00:02:52,879
The remaining two can be used with uranium-238, a fertile material.

34
00:02:54,339 --> 00:02:58,439
When an atom of uranium-238 absorbs one of these of these spare neutrons,

35
00:02:58,539 --> 00:03:02,240
it becomes plutonium-239.

36
00:03:04,639 --> 00:03:10,079
Now, if this is managed efficiently, we end up with more fissionable fuel than we started with.

37
00:03:12,299 --> 00:03:19,860
Perhaps the real story of the breeder begins here, what we call a vast storehouse of energy waiting to be tapped.

38
00:03:20,639 --> 00:03:24,039
What I'm leaning on is a canister of uranium hexafluoride.

39
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:27,819
This depleted uranium left over from the nation's weapons program

40
00:03:27,919 --> 00:03:32,739
and the enrichment process used to make fuel for today's conventional light-water reactors

41
00:03:32,839 --> 00:03:36,340
that now produce about 12% of the nation's electricity.

42
00:03:36,859 --> 00:03:40,799
This U-238 is now being stored at three government reservations,

43
00:03:41,539 --> 00:03:45,740
some 20,000 cylinders containing 280,000 tons of uranium.

44
00:03:46,319 --> 00:03:52,180
It is already mined and waiting to be used, without strip mining or otherwise disturbing the land.

45
00:03:52,539 --> 00:03:56,659
This existing stockpile of depleted uranium can't be used in today's reactors.

46
00:03:57,299 --> 00:03:59,459
They use enriched uranium-235.

47
00:04:00,519 --> 00:04:03,919
But in a breeder reactor, this leftover uranium-238

48
00:04:04,019 --> 00:04:07,799
is equivalent to the estimated oil resources of the entire world.

49
00:04:08,539 --> 00:04:11,959
This source alone could provide our total electrical energy needs

50
00:04:12,059 --> 00:04:13,039
for several centuries.

51
00:04:13,759 --> 00:04:16,019
But there's more than that to the breeder story.

52
00:04:16,679 --> 00:04:19,779
If you take the uranium we know for sure is in the ground

53
00:04:19,879 --> 00:04:25,319
and utilize this in the breeder, the energy content would be larger than all of the world's supply

54
00:04:25,419 --> 00:04:27,620
of coal, oil, and gas.

55
00:04:27,919 --> 00:04:32,819
It's no wonder, then, that virtually every major industrial nation in the world

56
00:04:32,919 --> 00:04:35,819
is vigorously pushing ahead with their own breeders.

57
00:04:35,919 --> 00:04:40,819
Great Britain, France, Japan, Soviet Russia, and West Germany

58
00:04:40,919 --> 00:04:42,819
all have operating breeder reactors.

59
00:04:42,919 --> 00:04:46,819
Belgium, the Netherlands, Italy, and Luxembourg

60
00:04:46,919 --> 00:04:48,819
are taking part in breeder ventures.

61
00:04:48,919 --> 00:04:53,819
The French plan to have a commercial-sized breeder in operation by 1983.

62
00:04:54,919 --> 00:05:01,459
In all, there are nine breeder reactors now operating, four more under construction, and nine others planned.

63
00:05:01,559 --> 00:05:07,459
Of these 22 breeders, Soviet Russia, which is the largest oil-producing nation in the world,

64
00:05:07,559 --> 00:05:09,459
accounts for five.

65
00:05:09,559 --> 00:05:11,459
They're planning for the future.

66
00:05:11,559 --> 00:05:15,459
All these nations realize the vast potential of the breeder

67
00:05:15,559 --> 00:05:20,459
and that time is running out on the age of cheap fossil fuels.

68
00:05:20,559 --> 00:05:26,459
Meanwhile, the United States has only one small electricity-producing breeder.

69
00:05:26,559 --> 00:05:33,459
Utilities with long experience in nuclear energy see breeder reactors as the next generation of nuclear power plants.

70
00:05:33,559 --> 00:05:42,459
With light-water reactors and then breeders, the U.S. could be assured of safe, clean, reliable, and economical nuclear power

71
00:05:42,559 --> 00:05:46,459
to meet the energy needs of this country well beyond the foreseeable future.

72
00:05:46,559 --> 00:05:52,459
Many people are not aware that the breeder concept dates back to the very beginning of the nuclear era.

73
00:05:52,559 --> 00:05:58,459
Nobel Prize-winning physicist Enrico Fermi, who pioneered the development of nuclear energy,

74
00:05:58,559 --> 00:06:02,459
predicted in 1945 that the nation that develops the breeder reactor

75
00:06:02,559 --> 00:06:05,459
will have a great competitive advantage.

76
00:06:05,559 --> 00:06:12,459
That's because the breeder, in a core like this model, can extend the energy available from our present uranium resources

77
00:06:12,559 --> 00:06:15,459
from 50 or so years to hundreds of years.

78
00:06:15,559 --> 00:06:19,459
For a breeder can deliver 60 times as much electricity from uranium

79
00:06:19,559 --> 00:06:21,459
as conventional nuclear reactors.

80
00:06:21,559 --> 00:06:24,459
The U.S. has almost 35 years' experience in breeders.

81
00:06:25,560 --> 00:06:29,460
Clementine, an experimental reactor, began operation in 1946.

82
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:33,460
The first nuclear power plant to produce electricity was a breeder.

83
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:39,460
The four light bulbs you see glowing here were illuminated by breeder power back in 1951.

84
00:06:39,560 --> 00:06:45,460
The plant was called EBR-1 for experimental breeder reactor number one.

85
00:06:45,560 --> 00:06:50,460
Its successor, EBR-2, began operating in 1963.

86
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:56,460
It's in its 17th year of successful operation and is still producing electricity.

87
00:06:56,560 --> 00:07:01,460
This year in Richland, Washington, the Fast Flux Test Facility went into operation.

88
00:07:01,560 --> 00:07:04,460
This is the largest breeder test reactor in the world.

89
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:09,460
This long experience underscores the fact that breeder technology is not new,

90
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:13,460
but it does have to be demonstrated in a utility environment.

91
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:17,460
This is why utilities from across the country, in partnership with the government,

92
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:20,460
want to build and operate the Clinch River Plant.

93
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:22,460
Project Management Corporation General Manager,

94
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:24,460
Mr. Bill Roth.

95
00:07:24,560 --> 00:07:25,460
Hello, Calvin.

96
00:07:25,560 --> 00:07:26,460
Hello, Bill.

97
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:27,460
Your progress, Bill.

98
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:28,460
How far along have you come?

99
00:07:28,560 --> 00:07:31,460
Calvin, since 1972, the project has come a long way.

100
00:07:31,560 --> 00:07:36,460
The research and development for the project is about 90% complete.

101
00:07:36,560 --> 00:07:40,460
The design is 80% complete.

102
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:44,460
About $80 million worth of equipment has already been delivered.

103
00:07:44,560 --> 00:07:49,460
The value of major components and test items on order is over half a billion dollars.

104
00:07:49,560 --> 00:07:55,460
The total investment in the project by government and industry now stands at over $850 million.

105
00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:57,460
And you're building more components every day.

106
00:07:57,560 --> 00:07:58,460
That's right.

107
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:05,460
The project has now over $250 million worth of components completed

108
00:08:05,560 --> 00:08:08,460
or in the fabrication pipeline.

109
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:10,460
Thank you, Mr. Roth.

110
00:08:10,560 --> 00:08:15,460
Now let's look at some specific examples of our progress in component procurement and fabrication.

111
00:08:15,560 --> 00:08:19,460
The $22.6 million reactor vessel for the Clinch River project

112
00:08:19,560 --> 00:08:21,460
was completed six months ago.

113
00:08:21,560 --> 00:08:26,460
That's a lot of costs ahead of schedule and $2.7 million underestimated cost.

114
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:30,460
Standing over five stories high and weighing 505 tons,

115
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:36,460
the stainless steel vessel will contain the nuclear core that is the heart of the power plant.

116
00:08:36,560 --> 00:08:41,460
The three intermediate heat exchangers for the Clinch River project are nearing completion.

117
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:44,460
All three are scheduled for shipment later this year.

118
00:08:44,560 --> 00:08:48,460
The three units will cost almost $40 million.

119
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:52,460
The drive systems for the main sodium pumps have also moved along over the past year.

120
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:56,460
The huge 45-ton drive motor is now in Oak Ridge.

121
00:09:10,560 --> 00:09:13,460
Other major components have also been delivered.

122
00:09:13,560 --> 00:09:17,460
These include components such as the protected water storage tank,

123
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:21,460
the pump drive system, the heat exchanger guard vessels,

124
00:09:21,560 --> 00:09:25,460
the reactor guard vessel, the pump guard vessel,

125
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:30,460
the in-vessel transfer machine, the plug drive and control system,

126
00:09:30,560 --> 00:09:33,460
and the primary sodium overflow vessel.

127
00:09:33,560 --> 00:09:37,460
Now let's take a look at the Clinch River project's technical merit.

128
00:09:37,560 --> 00:09:41,460
Over the past several years, numerous independent studies have taken a close look

129
00:09:41,560 --> 00:09:43,460
at the Clinch River project.

130
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:47,460
The latest is this report by the General Accounting Office.

131
00:09:47,560 --> 00:09:51,460
The studies have found that the Clinch River project's is sound and up-to-date.

132
00:09:51,560 --> 00:09:57,460
Our engineers have incorporated the most advanced breeder technology available in the United States and abroad,

133
00:09:57,560 --> 00:10:04,460
and the design has been constantly updated to keep it at the forefront of breeder reactor plant development.

134
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:14,460
A good example is its core design.

135
00:10:14,560 --> 00:10:17,460
A heterogeneous core design was adopted just last year.

136
00:10:17,560 --> 00:10:20,460
The design improves the breeding capability of the reactor.

137
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:24,460
The design is evidence of U.S. leadership in breeder technology.

138
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:29,460
Core designs based on alternate fuel cycles have also been developed.

139
00:10:29,560 --> 00:10:36,460
This means that the Clinch River breeder project could be utilized in the nation's ongoing liquid metal fast breeder reactor program

140
00:10:36,560 --> 00:10:40,460
as a testbed to examine proliferation, limiting concepts,

141
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:44,460
and advanced fuel designs for future power plants.

142
00:10:44,560 --> 00:10:46,460
Multiplexing is another innovation.

143
00:10:46,560 --> 00:10:51,460
This modern data communication system has proven its reliability

144
00:10:51,560 --> 00:10:56,460
in the telephone, petrochemical, military, and aerospace industries.

145
00:10:56,560 --> 00:11:00,460
Multiplexing makes it possible for one cable to carry as many signals

146
00:11:00,560 --> 00:11:03,460
as are ordinarily carried by 8,000 cables.

147
00:11:03,560 --> 00:11:08,460
The system will save 1.6 million feet of cable in the plant and reduce costs.

148
00:11:08,560 --> 00:11:13,460
Safety has been a paramount consideration in the design of the Clinch River project.

149
00:11:13,560 --> 00:11:20,460
As part of the continuing work on safety assurance, a comprehensive integrated review of key plant systems began last April

150
00:11:20,560 --> 00:11:25,460
to examine the plant design in light of the lessons learned at Three Mile Island.

151
00:11:25,560 --> 00:11:29,460
The full-scale mock-up of the main control room for the Clinch River plant

152
00:11:29,560 --> 00:11:33,460
is now installed in a building near the project office headquarters in Oak Ridge.

153
00:11:33,560 --> 00:11:38,460
Engineers are presently employing the model to check out the man-machine interfaces

154
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:42,460
to ensure that the operators can perform control plant procedures

155
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:44,460
with optimum efficiency and effectiveness.

156
00:11:45,560 --> 00:11:50,460
Another model, some 1,000 miles away, is also playing an important role

157
00:11:50,560 --> 00:11:52,460
in the development of the Clinch River plant.

158
00:11:52,560 --> 00:11:56,460
Under direction of the project office, this scale model of the plant

159
00:11:56,560 --> 00:12:00,460
is being built at the Burns and Rowe facility in Oroville, New Jersey.

160
00:12:00,560 --> 00:12:03,460
This is one of over 300 modules under construction.

161
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:07,460
The model is currently being used to establish final arrangements

162
00:12:07,560 --> 00:12:10,460
of piping and components to assure correct placements.

163
00:12:10,560 --> 00:12:14,460
The model will later be used to train employees in the operation of the plant.

164
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:19,460
The fabrication of components, our advanced technology,

165
00:12:19,560 --> 00:12:23,460
the state of our readiness to address licensing issues,

166
00:12:23,560 --> 00:12:27,460
all indicate very clearly the accomplishments of the Clinch River project.

167
00:12:27,560 --> 00:12:34,460
But the payoff for the American consumer in terms of energy availability for the future is here.

168
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:41,460
And here, where the plant to demonstrate the nation's breeder will be built.

169
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:44,460
The breeder is an insurance policy to preserve our energy options.

170
00:12:45,560 --> 00:12:49,460
And it's an important step toward energy independence.
